---
functor: 1
title: object, sym, let
---

set heading(numbering: none)

A declaration is one line. It works from its line to the end of the file. Write declarations after the front matter.

# object

`object vec: F, v, u` says that F, v and u are vectors. This is the meaning layer. Their look comes from one style rule, so you do not write `F.arrow` in each place.

```fn
object vec: F, v
set vec(style: bold)

$F = m a$ and $v = frac(dd x, dd t)$.
```

Today `vec` is the only kind for `object`. Own kinds come with `kind` in phase 1.

# sym

`sym csum = sum.circle` gives a recipe a short name. A recipe is one base and its operations, with no spaces.

```fn
sym csum = sum.circle
sym dint = integral.x2
sym RR = R.bb

$csum_C F + dint_D f + x in RR$
```

A `sym` that has the name of a built-in symbol hides it, and the compiler gives a warning. A `sym` can add more operations: `csum.small`.

# let

`let n = 3` makes a value. Write `{n}` in text to show it. A value is a number or text in quotes: `let name = "Ada"`. Values do not work in math yet.

```fn
let n = 3

There are {n} bodies.
```

# set

`set` lines are style rules. See [Document settings](document/settings.html) and [All set rules](document/set-rules.html).

# Not yet

`use`, `kind`, `style` and `default` are reserved for phase 1. The compiler refuses them with a clear message.
